By Pythagoras on right triangle $ABD,$
\[AB^2 = AD^2 + BD^2.\]By Pythagoras on right triangles $ACD$ and $BCD,$
\begin{align*}
AD^2 &= AC^2 - CD^2, \\
BD^2 &= BC^2 - CD^2,
\end{align*}so
\begin{align*}
\cos \angle ACB &= \frac{AC^2 + BC^2 - AB^2}{2 \cdot AC \cdot BC} \\
&= \frac{AC^2 + BC^2 - (AD^2 + BD^2)}{2 \cdot AC \cdot BC} \\
&= \frac{(AC^2 - AD^2) + (BC^2 - BD^2)}{2 \cdot AC \cdot BC} \\
&= \frac{2 \cdot CD^2}{2 \cdot AC \cdot BC} \\
&= \frac{CD}{AC} \cdot \frac{CD}{BC} \\
&= (\sin \angle CAD)(\sin \angle CBD) \\
&= \boxed{xy}.
\end{align*}

question:
Given 5 colors to choose from, how many ways can we color the four unit squares of a $2\times 2$ board, given that two colorings are considered the same if one is a rotation of the other? (Note that we can use the same color for more than one square.)
